WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.620
- This windswept hilltop
is Old Sarum in Wiltshire.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:05.490
It's an enormous Iron Age hillfort

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:07.830
and... you know what?
Actually, the best way to see it

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.943
is not from down here.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:15.499
This is one of the most important
historical sites in Southern England.

00:00:15.499 --> 00:00:19.300
An Iron Age fortification
from more than 2000 years ago.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.547
Then in the 11th century, a castle
was built for William the Conqueror

00:00:22.547 --> 00:00:24.760
and the Catholic church
built a cathedral here.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.250
You can see the foundations
of all those surviving centuries later.

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:30.710
This was a thriving bit of England.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:33.220
But it fell from grace.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:36.065
There's a lot of complicated reasons why,
but one of the main ones is:

00:00:36.065 --> 00:00:40.400
a windswept hilltop
is a terrible place for a cathedral.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.370
- Much as that looks impressive

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:44.830
and you can dominate a landscape

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:46.450
that brings a number of problems with it.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:49.780
Supplies of water from wells
were just not adequate

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.190
to support a large population.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.000
Even to the extent that they
actually needed to buy

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.320
the water that they needed to drink.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:57.640
When the weather isn't good,

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.520
the wind is blowing over,
the rain is coming at you,

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:04.180
when thunderstorms are going,
it's a very, very bad place.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.200
- As well as that,
the local bishop, Roger,

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.570
fell out with the new king Stephen,

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:08.770
and that's in a period of history

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:12.870
when "falling out with the king"
was very serious indeed.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.210
- Bishop Roger falls out with the king.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.080
He falls out with pretty much everyone.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:22.840
That is the price you pay for being active
within politics and within religion

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.220
and he plays the game at very high levels.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.210
And he does come crashing down.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.420
There's also an institutional problem.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.900
The king's men in the castle and
the bishop's men around the cathedral,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.650
you get a few references to conflict there

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:40.240
where, for example,
a procession of clergy was stopped

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.480
by some of the king's
soldiers behaving churlishly.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.373
They would easily go, look,
we don't want anymore of this.

00:01:46.373 --> 00:01:49.345
Begging the pope to release
them from all of this,

00:01:49.345 --> 00:01:50.625
and the pope saying absolutely,

00:01:50.625 --> 00:01:53.643
you can have somewhere that will be
all your own, down at the bottom.

00:01:53.643 --> 00:01:55.560
And giving them licence for it.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.632
- So the clergy moved a couple of miles away
to a new cathedral,

00:01:58.632 --> 00:02:01.900
in a place that would eventually
become known as Salisbury.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.930
The buildings here started
to fall into disrepair

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:07.350
and the once-busy town
declined and declined and declined.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:11.150
But despite that, for some reason,
the landowners of Old Sarum

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:14.290
were invited to send two
representatives to Parliament.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:15.640
This was the 14th century, now,

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.100
when the rules of Parliament
were still being written.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.902
And the reason why they were invited is...

00:02:20.902 --> 00:02:22.629
a bit of a mystery.

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:25.306
- Historians still aren't sure.

00:02:25.306 --> 00:02:30.440
One of the things that modern archaeology
is starting to tell us quite a bit about

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:34.990
is whether there was stuff going on
outside the hillfort on lower ground

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:36.700
that we don't know about,

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:40.720
that's nearer in to Old Sarum
than the new city of Salisbury.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:42.430
There's a fairly strong likelihood

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:45.660
that while the hillfort itself
had been abandoned

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.680
there was a bit more
settlement further down.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.927
And probably in the later middle ages

00:02:50.927 --> 00:02:54.400
it wouldn't have been regarded
as such a complete nonsense

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.610
of two MPs being elected
for a place with no population at all.

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:01.000
- So this hill had two
members of Parliament.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.290
And that didn't change for centuries.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:05.240
It didn't change as the
population dwindled,

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:07.120
it didn't change when King Henry VIII

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.470
signed over possession
of all the buildings

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:11.580
to a local official who
ordered them dismantled

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.480
and used for building material.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.140
By the 17th century, no one lived here.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:19.840
But you didn't have to live here to vote.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.990
Anyone who was nominated by the landowner

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.381
as being one of the tenants in this area,

00:03:24.381 --> 00:03:25.553
that was enough.

00:03:25.553 --> 00:03:28.460
And of course they voted
for the person who'd nominated them.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:31.770
So this hill became a rotten borough.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.990
If you owned the land,
then you and a friend,

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:37.525
or whichever two people you chose,
had seats in Parliament.

00:03:37.525 --> 00:03:41.000
Representing basically just yourself.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.354
And if you were particularly
rich and well connected

00:03:43.354 --> 00:03:45.920
you might own a couple
of other boroughs nearby

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:47.660
which would elect your friends.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.660
Meanwhile, places like
the city of Manchester

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.957
eventually growing to a
quarter of a million people

00:03:51.957 --> 00:03:55.175
had no one representing
them at all.

00:03:55.175 --> 00:03:56.720
For centuries.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.480
By the 19th century, it was clear
that something had to be done,

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.820
but how do you change something like that?

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:06.463
How do you convince people with
power to give up that power?

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:12.870
Public outrage. Radicals. Protests,
including one where 60,000 people

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.320
were attacked by the military

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.200
which came to be known
as the Peterloo Massacre.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.800
In May 1832, riots broke out

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:23.480
when the third attempt at a reform bill
was blocked in Parliament.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:27.150
And Britain likely came
close to revolution.

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:28.370
After all, the French Revolution

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:32.550
was still very much in, uh,
living memory then.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.500
In short, the reformers won.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.920
And historians can argue
about how much of that

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:39.810
was political wrangling by
the people who gained power,

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:43.773
and how much of that was the
very real threat of violent revolution.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.800
When the dust settled,
the law was changed,

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:48.440
the rotten boroughs were abolished,

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:51.410
and there were 130 new seats
in Parliament.

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:53.870
Voting was still only for men
with land or money though.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:57.970
It took until 1918 for that to become
"all men and some women",

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:00.640
and it was 1928 before it was "all adults".

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.340
These days parliamentary boundaries

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:06.110
are drawn by an independent group
based on population.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:09.380
This hill is managed by a charity
called English Heritage,

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:13.850
and one Member of Parliament for Salisbury
represents this whole area

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:16.512
and the roughly 70,000 people
who live in it.

00:05:16.512 --> 00:05:20.408
It's important to remember
that history is fractal.

00:05:20.408 --> 00:05:23.990
You could spend days
or years or a lifetime

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:28.341
studying everything that happened
over those few days in 1832.

00:05:28.341 --> 00:05:32.170
Or you could sum up
centuries in a few minutes.

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:35.970
But there's one thing that
Old Sarum shows very clearly,

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:39.930
it is extremely difficult to
convince people with power

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:41.453
to give it up willingly.

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:44.500
Thanks to English Heritage for
letting me film at Old Sarum,

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:47.750
you can find out more about them
and about visiting here on their website.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:49.300
The link is in the description.

